  "text": "To solve this problem, we'll use Vieta's formulas and manipulate the given information about the roots of the polynomials $P(x)$ and $Q(x)$. \n\nFirst, let's recall Vieta's formulas for a cubic polynomial $x^3 + ax^2 + bx + c = 0$ with roots $p, q, r$:\n1. $p + q + r = -a$\n2. $pq + qr + rp = b$\n3. $pqr = -c$\n\nGiven that $r, s,$ and $t$ are the roots of $P(x)$, and $r-2, s+5,$ and $t-1$ are the roots of $Q(x)$, we can write:\n1. $r + s + t = -a$\n2. $rs + rt + st = b$\n3. $rst = -c$\n\nBut there's also a special condition for $Q(x)$, which is that we have an additional constant term of $120$. Therefore, we can write:\n\\[ Q(x) = (x - (r-2)) + (x - (s+5)) + (x - (t-1)) + 120 \\]\n\\[ Q(x) = 3x^3 + (-3r - 3s - 3t) + (122 - 3r) + (122 - 3s) + (122 - 3t) \\]\nFrom this, we can see that the coefficients of $Q(x)$ match the conditions above. Thus, we can write:\n\\[ 3(r + s + t) = -3( -a) \\]\n\\[ 122 - 3(r + s + t) = b \\]\n\\[ 122 - 3(r + s + t) = c \\]\n\nFrom the first equation of Vieta for $P(x)$, we have $r + s + t = -a$. Substituting this into the above equations, we get:\n1. $3(-a) = -3(-a)$\n2. $122 - 3(-a) = b$\n3. $122 - 3(-a) = c$\n\nTherefore, we can simplify the equations as follows:\n1. $a = a$\n2. $122 + 3a = b$\n3. $122 + 3a = c$\n\nFor which values of $c$ does this hold true? Since the first equation is always true and gives us no new information, we must rely on the other two equations to find $c$.\n\nWe see that $c = 122 + 3a$. Since $a$ can be any real number, $c$ must also be any real number greater than or equal to $122$ because $3a$ will always be positive. Thus, $c$ can be any real number ranging from $122$ to infinity.\n\nTherefore, the final answer is:\n\\[\n\\boxed{122}\n\\]",
